    <!-- ─── Intro ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <section id="start">
      <div class="container">
        <p class="eyebrow">The flow</p>
        <h1>One field, all the way down.</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          This page follows <code>email</code> — a single field on a single Model — from the
          line that declares it to the input a user types into. Every stop names the concept
          doing the work, and every <strong>Boundary</strong> it crosses is marked, because
          the Boundaries are where the interesting things happen.
        </p>
        <p class="lede" style="margin-top: 0.9rem">
          Three realms, in order: <strong>Data</strong> holds the Model,
          <strong>API</strong> exposes the Service, <strong>UI</strong> binds a Resource.
          Nothing here is a restatement of anything before it.
        </p>

        <div class="note">
          <b>On the schema.</b> Treat <code>.lite</code> as Prisma with two extra ideas — a
          <b>Gate</b> and <b>policies</b>. Everything else on this page is unaffected by how
          deep the Data realm goes.
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ─── The flow ────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <section>
      <div class="container flow">
        <!-- 1 · Model -->
        <div class="stop" id="data">
          <div class="rail">
            <span class="badge-realm">Realm 01 · Data</span>
            <span class="noun">Model</span>
            <span class="where">db/schema.lite</span>
          </div>
          <div class="body">
            <p>
              The Model is the seed. It states what exists, what rules govern it, and who may
              touch it — and it is the only place any of that is written.
            </p>
            <pre class="code">{@html sample.S0}</pre>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Boundary 1 -->
        <div class="boundary">
          <span class="tag">Boundary · Data ⇄ API</span>
          <div class="seam">db.$setAuth(user) &nbsp;·&nbsp; db.asSystem()</div>
          <p>
            A Service never holds the root client. Every call gets a client scoped to the
            caller, and that scoping is what makes the Gate and the policies real: the level
            check runs, and <code>@@allow</code> is compiled into the
            <code>WHERE</code> clause before the query is executed. <code>asSystem()</code> is
            the declared bypass for background work — and it does not pass
            <code>LOCKED</code>.
          </p>
          <div class="crosses">
            <span>ctx.locals.db</span><span>Gate → 401/403</span><span>policy → WHERE</span
            ><span>field rules → 400</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- 2 · Service -->
        <div class="stop" id="service">
          <div class="rail">
            <span class="badge-realm">Realm 02 · API</span>
            <span class="noun">Service</span>
            <span class="where">api/src/services/<br />leads.service.ts</span>
          </div>
          <div class="body">
            <p>
              The Service is what the outside world can ask for. Here is the whole file — it
              is empty because everything in it would be a restatement of the Model.
            </p>
            <pre class="code">{@html sample.S1}</pre>
            <p>
              That gives <code>find get create update patch remove</code> at
              <code>/api/leads</code>, a <code>400</code> when <code>email</code> is not an
              email, a <code>401</code> below <code>READER</code>, and a list filtered by the
              policy — none of it written, all of it read from the Model at call time.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- 3 · Context -->
        <div class="stop" id="context">
          <div class="rail">
            <span class="badge-realm">Realm 02 · API</span>
            <span class="noun">Context</span>
            <span class="where">ctx</span>
          </div>
          <div class="body">
            <p>
              One call, one <strong>Context</strong>. It is the single argument every Hook and
              every Service method receives, and its parts have different rules — which is the
              part worth learning early.
            </p>
            <div class="table-wrap">
              <table class="k">
                <thead>
                  <tr>
                    <th>On the Context</th>
                    <th>Is</th>
                    <th>Propagates to an internal call?</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr>
                    <td><code>ctx.auth.user</code></td>
                    <td>Who is calling. Frozen.</td>
                    <td>Yes — identity must stay consistent</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td><code>ctx.client</code></td>
                    <td>ip, user-agent, headers. Read-only.</td>
                    <td>Yes — <code>&#123;&#125;</code> when there is no real caller</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td><code>ctx.query</code></td>
                    <td><strong>Filters only.</strong> Becomes the <code>WHERE</code>.</td>
                    <td>Per call</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td><code>ctx.directives</code></td>
                    <td><strong>Shaping only</strong> — limit, offset, orderBy, select.</td>
                    <td>Per call</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td><code>ctx.locals</code></td>
                    <td>Scratch space for this call. Fresh <code>&#123;&#125;</code>.</td>
                    <td class="no">No — a sub-call cannot reach its caller</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td><code>ctx.result</code> / <code>ctx.error</code></td>
                    <td>Null before, populated after.</td>
                    <td>Per call</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
            <div class="note">
              <b><code>$</code> is wire syntax and stops at the Boundary.</b>
              <code>?$limit=20</code> arrives as <code>ctx.directives.limit</code>; nothing
              inside the framework ever reads a <code>$</code>. Conflating the two is what
              made <code>?limit=1</code> a filter on a column named "limit" — zero rows, no
              error.
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- 4 · Hooks -->
        <div class="stop" id="hooks">
          <div class="rail">
            <span class="badge-realm">Realm 02 · API</span>
            <span class="noun">Hook</span>
            <span class="where">Chain of<br />Responsibility</span>
          </div>
          <div class="body">
            <p>
              A <strong>Hook</strong> is how you step into a call without owning it. Four
              phases, declared per method or for <code>all</code>, and they are the same four
              phases you will meet again in the UI realm.
            </p>
            <pre class="code">{@html sample.S2}</pre>
            <p>
              Your Hooks run <strong>first</strong>, then the ones derived from the Model — so
              <code>stampOwner</code> can complete the record before the Gate check and the
              field rules judge it. Setting <code>ctx.result</code> in a
              <code>before</code> Hook ends the chain early; throwing sends it to
              <code>error</code>.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- 5 · Plugins -->
        <div class="stop" id="plugins">
          <div class="rail">
            <span class="badge-realm">Realm 02 · API</span>
            <span class="noun">Plugin</span>
            <span class="where">api/src/app.ts</span>
          </div>
          <div class="body">
            <p>
              A <strong>Plugin</strong> attaches a capability to the application and claims a
              name on it. Everything a Hook can then reach for — the queue, the mailer, the
              outbound boundary — arrived this way.
            </p>
            <pre class="code">{@html sample.S3}</pre>
            <div class="table-wrap">
              <table class="k">
                <thead>
                  <tr>
                    <th>The contract</th>
                    <th>When</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr>
                    <td><code>register(app)</code></td>
                    <td>
                      Immediately, and <strong>synchronously</strong> — claim your name with
                      <code>app.provide()</code>
                    </td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td><code>boot(app)</code></td>
                    <td>At startup, async — connections, migrations, workers</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td><code>ready(app)</code> / <code>shutdown(app)</code></td>
                    <td>After listening / on the way down</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td><code>requires: ['mailer']</code></td>
                    <td>Checked once before any <code>boot</code> — ordering, declared</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- 6 · Events & Channels -->
        <div class="stop" id="events">
          <div class="rail">
            <span class="badge-realm">Realm 02 · API</span>
            <span class="noun">Event · Channel</span>
            <span class="where">channel: 'leads'</span>
          </div>
          <div class="body">
            <p>
              An <strong>Event</strong> is what happened. A <strong>Channel</strong> is who
              hears about it. A mutation is announced in exactly one place — the call itself —
              so no Hook and no method can forget to publish.
            </p>
            <pre class="code">{@html sample.S4}</pre>
            <div class="note">
              <b>Omitting <code>channel:</code> means no broadcast at all</b> — there is no
              default. And a Channel named as a plain string reaches every connection on it:
              policies are enforced on <em>read</em>, not on broadcast. Scope it with the
              function form before shipping anything per-tenant.
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Boundary 2 -->
        <div class="boundary">
          <span class="tag">Boundary · API ⇄ UI</span>
          <div class="seam">
            bridge.toContext() &nbsp;·&nbsp; toResponse() &nbsp;·&nbsp; the envelope
          </div>
          <p>
            Going out, this is where a Context becomes an HTTP response and where the result
            gets its envelope — <code>kind: 'single' | 'list'</code>, with a list keeping the
            envelope and a single unwrapping. Coming in, it is where <code>$limit</code>
            becomes a directive and a thrown value becomes a status. The UI realm never
            guesses a shape, because one module owns both ends of it.
          </p>
          <div class="crosses">
            <span>envelope</span><span>$ → directives</span><span>toFrameworkError()</span
            ><span>JSON Schema</span><span>Channel events</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- 7 · Resource -->
        <div class="stop" id="resource">
          <div class="rail">
            <span class="badge-realm">Realm 03 · UI</span>
            <span class="noun">Resource</span>
            <span class="where">web/src/routes/<br />leads/index.mesa</span>
          </div>
          <div class="body">
            <p>
              A <strong>Resource</strong> is how the UI binds to a Service. It carries the
              data — and, because the Model's rules crossed the Boundary with it, it carries
              the rules too.
            </p>
            <pre class="code">{@html sample.S5}</pre>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- 8 · Resource hooks + events -->
        <div class="stop">
          <div class="rail">
            <span class="badge-realm">Realm 03 · UI</span>
            <span class="noun">Hook · Event</span>
            <span class="where">the same four phases</span>
          </div>
          <div class="body">
            <p>
              The UI realm's Hooks are the API realm's Hooks. Same names, same order, same
              Context idea — learned once.
            </p>
            <pre class="code">{@html sample.S6}</pre>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- 9 · The field -->
        <div class="stop" id="form">
          <div class="rail">
            <span class="badge-realm">Realm 03 · UI</span>
            <span class="noun">The field</span>
            <span class="where">&lt;Input /&gt;</span>
          </div>
          <div class="body">
            <p>
              The end of the journey. The input knows it is an email, knows it is required,
              knows its maximum length, and knows when it is wrong — and none of that is
              written here.
            </p>
            <pre class="code">{@html sample.S7}</pre>
            <p>
              <code>coerce</code> turns the string every control produces into the type the
              Model declared. <code>validate</code> checks it against the same rules the
              Service will apply. If both are switched off, nothing breaks — the server still
              says no. They only change <strong>where</strong> the answer arrives.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Boundary 3 -->
        <div class="boundary">
          <span class="tag">Boundary · back the way it came</span>
          <div class="seam">create → Service → Gate → policy → row</div>
          <p>
            Pressing Save sends the record back through every stop in reverse, and every check
            runs again on the server side of the Boundary. The browser's copy of the rules
            never had authority — it had a preview.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ─── What was written ────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <section id="tally">
      <div class="container">
        <h2>What was written, and what was read</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          The whole path above, tallied. Everything in the right column exists because of a
          line in the left one.
        </p>

        <div class="two" style="margin-top: 1.5rem">
          <article class="card stack">
            <h3>Written by you</h3>
            <pre class="code">{@html sample.S8}</pre>
          </article>

          <article class="card stack">
            <h3>Read from the Model</h3>
            <div class="table-wrap">
              <table class="k">
                <tbody>
                  <tr>
                    <td>The migration</td>
                    <td>column, type, <code>UNIQUE</code></td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td>The Service</td>
                    <td>six operations at <code>/api/leads</code></td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td>The <code>400</code></td>
                    <td>from <code>@email</code>, <code>@length</code></td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td>The <code>401</code>/<code>403</code></td>
                    <td>from the Gate</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td>The <code>WHERE</code></td>
                    <td>from the policy</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td>The types</td>
                    <td>server and browser</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td><code>leads.fields.email</code></td>
                    <td>the browser's copy of the rule</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td>The input</td>
                    <td>type, required, maxlength</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </article>
        </div>

        <div class="cluster" style="margin-top: 1.75rem; gap: 0.75rem">
          <a class="btn primary raised" href="/#start">Get started</a>
          <a class="btn outlined" href="/#extend">The four ways to extend it</a>
          <a class="btn ghost" href="/landscape/">Where it came from</a>
          <span class="badge warning" title="Working, but APIs may still move">Alpha</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

<!-- The page's own closing note. The site footer is the layout's. -->
